## The five gating questions (build is blocked on these)

The absolute minimum to unblock the build. Everything else defaults.

### L1 — Primary capture device (phone or laptop?)
- **Options:** phone-first (voice-to-Telegram canonical, mobile in MVP) vs laptop-first (CLI canonical, mobile waits).
- **Jarvis position:** the binary is softer than framed — the MVP ships BOTH CLI and Telegram regardless (the 17:00 check-in needs the bot). So L1 really decides one thing: **is voice-note transcription in MVP or v1.0?** Declare laptop-primary, include Telegram text capture for free, add voice-note transcription to MVP only if paper captures mostly happen away from the desk.
- **Nick's answer:** _pending._

### L3 — Canonical store (SQLite / markdown / Postgres?)
- **Options:** SQLite canonical (architect flip) vs markdown canonical + SQLite read-model (original) vs Postgres (overkill).
- **Jarvis position:** **agree with the flip, no reservations.** SQLite canonical at `~/.claude/PAI/MEMORY/INKWELL/inkwell.db`, markdown daily-page export as a view. Single user means no merge problem, the agent writes constantly, "hand-editable" is a phantom benefit (the Telos files never get hand-edited either). This is the boring correct answer.
- **Nick's answer:** _pending._

### L4 — Agent autonomy boundary
- **Options:** propose-and-confirm (agent never silently mutates) vs autonomous (act-and-log).
- **Jarvis position:** **propose-and-confirm for the first 30 days**, with one boundary clarification — the rule binds **unprompted** mutation only. When Nick replies to the 17:00 check-in with "done A, roll B, drop C," applying those edits is instructed, not autonomy; the agent must not re-confirm what Nick just told it. Agent-initiated silent edits: forbidden. Edits Nick dictated in natural language: applied immediately.
- **Nick's answer:** _pending._

### L8 — Bake-off success metric (the done condition / headline ISC)
- **Architect proposal:** after 14 days of parallel use, Nick prefers Inkwell for ≥80% of new captures and reviews next-day items in Inkwell ≥90% of mornings. Miss either → kill, notebook won.
- **Jarvis position:** **accept 80/90 intent, fix the measurement mechanics.** Measuring "≥80% of captures went to Inkwell" requires tallying paper captures too, and instrumenting paper adds friction that rigs the bake-off. Cleaner instrument, same intent: **count notebook lines written per day (one glance at the page each evening, agent logs it at check-in); target a falling trend reaching ≤1 line/day by day 14**, plus the ≥90% next-day-review-in-Inkwell condition unchanged. Kill condition unchanged.
- **Nick's answer:** _pending._

### A4 — Relationship to the Algorithm's ISA/ISC system
- **Options:** parallel (both stores exist) / tasks subordinate to ISCs / tasks subsume ISCs for personal execution.
- **Jarvis position:** **agree with parallel for MVP**, but buy the hedge now — the schema carries a nullable `isc_ref` column from day one. Costs nothing at MVP and makes the v1.0 revisit (subordinate vs subsume) a feature decision rather than a data migration. This question decides whether Inkwell is "a todo app on top of PAI" or "the personal execution layer of the PAI Life OS."
- **Nick's answer:** _pending._

